<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">John D. Rockefeller</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1839">1839</a>–<a href="/years/1937">1937</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as John Davison Rockefeller Sr.</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">John D. Rockefeller was an American businessman and philanthropist, born in Upstate <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a>, who founded the Standard Oil Company in Cleveland, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ohio">Ohio</a>, in 1870 and ran it until 1897. By 1900 Standard Oil controlled about 90% of the nation's oil production. The company, criticised by writers such as Ida Tarbell, was ordered dismantled by the Supreme Court in 1911 and broken into 34 entities. Rockefeller became the country's first billionaire in 1916. In retirement he funded medicine, education and research through foundations, which helped nearly eradicate hookworm in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-south">American South</a>, and founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-chicago">University of Chicago</a> and Rockefeller University.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/john-d-rockefeller.jpg" alt="John D. Rockefeller" width="330" height="452" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Published by Scientific American Compiling Dep't, New York. 1907. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portrait_of_J._D._Rockefeller.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Restoration began in 1892 under the first conservator, Pierre de Nolhac, and <b>John D. Rockefeller</b>, Jr. gave $2,166,000 between 1925 and 1928.</blockquote><a href="/work/palace-of-versailles">Palace of Versailles · Survival &amp; Rediscovery</a></li></ul></section>
